<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="sparc:BE:32:default">
<!--
    Issue #862 (and part A of #815): on SPARC a call's return value was
    dropped and the argument register printed in its place.

        f1:  atoi(a0); return a0 + 1;
        f6:  atoll(a0); return CONCAT44(a1 + 1,...);   (o0:o1 read as the arguments)

    SparcV9_32.cspec lists no killedbycall registers, and its output rules
    switch off the automatic kill of the output registers, so %o0 crossed
    every call as an unknown effect (the value before the call) and never
    became the call's output.  The cspec now kills o0, o1 and fd0 (the
    output registers) at every call.  That alone turned a function's own
    long long into an int (widen printed return a0 >> 0x1f): SPARC's call
    p-code keeps a RETURN for a restore in the delay slot behind
    if (didrestore == 0), never taken, and that RETURN read %o1 as the
    call's clobber.  Return recovery now skips such a RETURN.

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (call output trials, return recovery).
    No option: a strict fix (cspec + never-reached RETURN skip).
    Source (clang 14 -fintegrated-as -fno-pic, sparc-linux-gnu; the calls are
    relocated to undefined stubs at 0x80000+):
        at 0x10000 (-O2):
        int f1(const char *s) { return atoi(s) + 1; }
        int f4(const char *s) { int r = atoi(s); use(r); return r * 3; }
        int f5(const char *s) { return atoi(s); }
        long long f6(const char *s) { return atoll(s) + 1; }
        void fl(float x, float *p) { *p = ff(x); }
        long long widen(int x) { ext(); return x; }
        at 0x20000 (-O0):
        int g0(const char *s) { int r = atoi(s); if (r > 5) use(r); return r; }
    Asserts #1-#6: each call's result is used where the code uses it.
    Assert #7: no call prints as a bare statement with its result dropped.
    Asserts #8-#9: widen keeps its long long return (o0:o1).
-->
